Not submitted upstream.
llama-ple-pipeline.patch, 2026-11-11. Base: llama.cpp master 7592b61d3 WITH llama-ple-prefetch-async.patch applied first
(both edit src/models/qwen4exp.cpp; this file only). Apply: git apply llama-ple-prefetch-async.patch && git apply llama-ple-pipeline.patch
OPT-IN, DEFAULT OFF: LLAMA_PLE_PIPELINE=1 (LLAMA_PLE_PIPELINE_THREADS, default 8).
What: the PLE n-gram embedding was a CPU GET_ROWS (IQ4_NL rows -> 4-byte floats, 42 MB per 4096-token ubatch) in the
graph's first split, plus a 42 MB upload. With the knob on, set_input copies the table's RAW rows of each token's heads
(90 B each; one token's 16 heads = 2,560 values, whole 256-value blocks) on 8 host threads into a [2560, n_tokens] input
of the table's type (5.9 MB per 4096 tokens), and the GPU dequantizes it with GET_ROWS over an iota index. The layout
and the dequant arithmetic (fp16 scale x int8 table value) are the same as stock. Falls back to stock when the table
is not host memory or its rows are not whole blocks.
Not an overlap: each llama_decode call here is one ubatch, the server gives llama the next call's tokens only
at that call, and the graph alloc waits for the GPU. So the gather is made cheap rather than hidden behind GPU work.
Correctness: greedy --temp 0, 4 prompts, bitwise identical to stock (-ncmoe 12, -ub 4096); greedy + checkpoint-restore
test bitwise identical to the same build without the knob; llama-eval-callback tensor sums identical through the whole
model; test-backend-ops GET_ROWS 216/216 on CUDA (incl. iq4_nl).
Measured (RTX 5090, a MoE GGUF (IQ1_M), -t 8 -b 4096 -ub 4096 --cache-ram 0, checkpoints on, with pinned-copy-stream v2,
llama-moe-prefill, llama-server-ckpt-min-head (=256), llama-ple-prefetch-async, GGML_CUDA_WS_SLOTS=24; cold chat text,
wall tok/s):
  -ncmoe 12, 10k (n=6): off 5,888 [5,829-5,917] -> on 6,012 [4,791-6,062] (5 of 6 reps above off's max; the outlier is a
    first rep with 879 major faults)
  -ncmoe 12, 2.7k (n=6): off 5,198 [3,521-5,334] -> on 4,923 [3,494-5,471] (no gain; both have the slow first request)
  -ncmoe 6, 10k (n=3): 5,935 -> 6,036;  -ncmoe 24, 10k (n=3): 5,308 -> 5,431;  2.7k unchanged at both
  BoltBeam time-budget, -ncmoe 12 10k: 1,693.6 -> 1,645.4 ms (host outside the GPU work 108.3 -> 73.9, idle 112.2 -> 98.9)
Caveat: about 2.3x the major faults per request (~470 vs ~210). The gather reads the mmap right after the async prefetch
is issued, so a cold page-cache state costs more here than in the stock path.

